-- title:
--   Odd traces vanish for an even quotient
-- statement:
--   Let $G$ be a finite abelian group. Let $a,d,h\in G$ satisfy $\operatorname{ord}(d+\langle h\rangle)=3$ and $\langle a,d,h\rangle=G$. Assume $|G/\langle h\rangle|$ is even. Write $S=\{a,a+d,a+d+h\}$ and $T=\{a,a-d,a-d+h\}$, and let $A_U(x,y)=1$ if $y-x\in U$ and $0$ otherwise, with rational entries. For every odd natural number $n$,
--
--   $$\operatorname{tr}(A_S^n)=\operatorname{tr}(A_T^n)=0.$$
--
--   This supplies vanishing odd walk traces from quotient parity.
-- source:
--   Alexandre Couret, https://github.com/alexcour/couret-finite-verifications/blob/65b828d9ffd016ce2a1b05403f303fa5f948c10f/research/correspondants_20261013/lean/CayleyTwoColourParity.lean#L365-L375, lines 365–375; source file SHA-256 d093cc5d0fb31526ef68d4b1d68ee24818c67ebec52adc75170199eecb1ea7a6; theorem Couret.CayleyDoublet.abelianThree_odd_traces_zero_of_even_quotient

